{{Documentation/Header}}
Template for creating a hidden HTML anchor (ID) on a page. Useful when you want to target a specific point on the page with a link (e.g. [[Mafumafu/Discography#Kagurairo Artifact|Kagurairo Artifact]]) but you don’t have or don’t want a visible heading at that exact spot.

==Purpose==
This template outputs a small, hidden HTML element with an id attribute suitable for linking to from within or between pages. It HTML-encodes the provided anchor name so the resulting id is safe for use in an element id.

==Syntax==
<pre>{{anchor
|name = AnchorName
|class = hide
}}</pre>

* <code>name</code> (required) — the anchor name (text used to build the element id).
* <code>class</code> (optional) — CSS class to apply to the generated &lt;div&gt;. Defaults to <code>hide</code>.

When invoked without a parameter, this template renders an empty anchor element with an empty id.

==Examples==
Basic usage (create an anchor called "Anchorname"):
<pre>{{anchor|Anchorname}}</pre>
…results in:
<pre>&lt;div id="Anchorname" class="hide"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</pre>

Usage with a name containing spaces (encoding illustrated):
<pre>{{anchor|Section 2 — Details}}</pre>
…results in a safe id such as:
<pre>&lt;div id="Section_2_%E2%80%94_Details" class="hide"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</pre>

Make the element visible (for debugging) by passing an empty <code>class</code>:
<pre>{{anchor|Anchorname|class=}}</pre>
…results in:
<pre>&lt;div id="Anchorname"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</pre>

==When to use==
* Use this template when you need a stable link target but don't want a visible heading.
* Use instead of manual &lt;div id="..."&gt; when you want automatic encoding and consistency.

==When NOT to use==
* Do not use this to create visible navigation elements — headings (==Heading==) or named spans inside content are better.
* Avoid having multiple anchors with the same resulting id on the same rendered page — duplicate ids can produce unpredictable scroll/target behavior.

==Troubleshooting / Notes==
* Duplicate IDs: if multiple transclusions or templates create the same anchor id on a single page, the browser may jump to the first occurrence only. Make anchor names unique.
* Encoding: certain characters (quotes, angle brackets, em-dashes, non-ASCII) are encoded by <nowiki>{{anchorencode}}</nowiki>. If you need predictable ASCII-only ids, pass an ASCII-safe name.
* Transclusion: If you transclude a page that contains anchors into multiple places, the id will appear multiple times on the final page—avoid this or use unique prefixes.
* Accessibility: anchors without visible text don't hurt accessibility for screen readers (they ignore empty elements), but ensure your target makes sense in context.

